From 57569ff82299ebaf2c8c122d081f4ef322432235 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: terminills <tdeters@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 20:11:54 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 629/638] aros: record both pinned-run wedge signatures on
 --aros-pin-workers

tset9 was post-mortemed (spinlock PI self-deadlock); tset7 was not. Its
serial ends with CPU 0's scheduler reporting the current task out of
stack limits with a name pointing into code - a corrupted ThisTask -
right after thread 18 was placed on CPU 1. Say so rather than fold it
into the decoded one.


---
 base/base_switches.cc | 24 ++++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/base/base_switches.cc b/base/base_switches.cc
index ee1e78763d..9d1ac431d8 100644
--- a/base/base_switches.cc
+++ b/base/base_switches.cc
@@ -206,17 +206,21 @@ const char kArosCpuLimit[] = "aros-cpu-limit";
 // on one core. Placement is the whole difference (see
 // aros-adt-src/CHROMIUM-SMP-ROADMAP.md).
 //
-// OFF BY DEFAULT, deliberately. The first run whose threads really landed on
+// OFF BY DEFAULT, deliberately. The first runs whose threads really landed on
 // CPUs 1-3 (2026-09-20, after the libpthread link fix in base/BUILD.gn made
-// the affinity attribute reach exec at all) wedged the guest within a minute:
-// exec's Wait() holds the task's own tc_SpinLock while it contends the
-// TaskWait list lock, and KrnSpinLock's priority-inheritance branch answers
-// contention with SetTaskPri(self), which takes that same non-recursive lock
-// (arch/all-pc/kernel/spinlock.c:68, rom/exec/settaskpri.c:79). Until that is
-// fixed in the kernel, switching this on trades a working single-core browser
-// for a hung multi-core one. Keeping it a flag means this build is never
-// worse than the last one, and multicore can be exercised the moment the
-// kernel side lands - without another rebuild.
+// the affinity attribute reach exec at all) wedged the guest within a minute,
+// twice, with two signatures. Decoded from a live post-mortem: exec's Wait()
+// holds the task's own tc_SpinLock while it contends the TaskWait list lock,
+// and KrnSpinLock's priority-inheritance branch answers contention with
+// SetTaskPri(self), which takes that same non-recursive lock
+// (arch/all-pc/kernel/spinlock.c:68, rom/exec/settaskpri.c:79). Not yet
+// decoded: CPU 0's scheduler reporting its current task "went out of stack
+// limits" with a Task whose name points into code, right after the 18th
+// thread was placed on CPU 1 - a corrupted ThisTask, not a stack overflow.
+// Until the kernel side is fixed, switching this on trades a working
+// single-core browser for a hung multi-core one. Keeping it a flag means this
+// build is never worse than the last one, and multicore can be exercised the
+// moment the kernel side lands - without another rebuild.
 const char kArosPinWorkers[] = "aros-pin-workers";
 
 // Let the thread pool choose WHICH worker to wake by the load on that worker's
